(5443821, 779497, 4226, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Do you really need a critique ? :)<br/> <br/>Love the overall blue tone to the image, well composed and exposed. The moving clouds add to the picture and ''MELBOURNE'' blending in is a nice touch.<br/> <br/>If I can have a wish list:  Not this wide a border, a 2x3 landscape image I would have liked more.  The water was a little more smooth on the left bottom corner. <br/> <br/>Overall an amazing image, a postcard that I would definitely I would consider buying.<br/> <br/>regards,<br/>Gaurawa</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Do you really need a critique ? :) Love the overall blue tone to the image, well composed and exposed. The moving clouds add to the picture and ''MELBOURNE'' blending in is a nice touch. If I can have a wish list:  Not this wide a border, a 2x3 landscape image I would have liked more.  The water was a little more smooth on the left bottom corner.  Overall an amazing image, a postcard that I would definitely I would consider buying. regards,Gaurawa', '2009-04-19 14:08:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(5444808, 779788, 41337, '<td valign="top">Very nice postcard but if it were me (for Philly) I would have gone with a shot of Santa getting booed, thrown at, that sort of thing. :P</td>', 'Very nice postcard but if it were me (for Philly) I would have gone with a shot of Santa getting booed, thrown at, that sort of thing. :P', '2009-04-20 02:56:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(5449741, 779200, 57275, '<td valign="top">No doubt!! incredible picture. Perfect timing, exposure, composition everything is just amazing in this picture. Just wanted to ask due to curiosity, since you have mentioned that you kept the camera on self timer so that you could have reached to take the position but there the surface was muddy and wet, so how did you manage to get rid off the foot marks that you made while reaching there. <br/> <br/> akshat!!</td>', 'No doubt!! incredible picture. Perfect timing, exposure, composition everything is just amazing in this picture. Just wanted to ask due to curiosity, since you have mentioned that you kept the camera on self timer so that you could have reached to take the position but there the surface was muddy and wet, so how did you manage to get rid off the foot marks that you made while reaching there.   akshat!!', '2009-04-23 07:19:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(5450057, 779200, 102386, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by akshut:</b></div><hr/><i> No doubt!! incredible picture. Perfect timing, exposure, composition everything is just amazing in this picture. Just wanted to ask due to curiosity, since you have mentioned that you kept the camera on self timer so that you could have reached to take the position but there the surface was muddy and wet, so how did you manage to get rid off the foot marks that you made while reaching there. <br/> <br/> akshat!! </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I didn''t do anything to get rid of prints, they just did not appear on the photo. I guess it''s probably a combination of the angle, the reflection and the wet sand.</td>', 'Originally posted by akshut: No doubt!! incredible picture. Perfect timing, exposure, composition everything is just amazing in this picture. Just wanted to ask due to curiosity, since you have mentioned that you kept the camera on self timer so that you could have reached to take the position but there the surface was muddy and wet, so how did you manage to get rid off the foot marks that you made while reaching there.   akshat!!   I didn''t do anything to get rid of prints, they just did not appear on the photo. I guess it''s probably a combination of the angle, the reflection and the wet sand.', '2009-04-23 15:40:53.000000', NULL, 0),
